## Sandboxes

Sandboxes support two deployment topologies:

- By default this chart deploys the sandbox runtime, `sandbox-host`, alongside LangSmith on dedicated KVM-capable nodes.
- With `sandboxes.external.enabled=true` the runtime is external: this release keeps the sandbox API and control plane but omits the runtime resources. Install the `langsmith-sandbox` chart in the sandbox cluster and give it an externally reachable LangSmith API endpoint plus a minimal existing Secret containing the shared sandbox service-auth key and callback-signing JWK.

When the clusters share a private network you trust, leave `sandboxes.external.hostIngressEndpoint` empty and allow the LangSmith cluster to reach sandbox node IPs on TCP port 19190; that traffic is plain HTTP and carries sandbox commands, files and credentials. Otherwise, enable the `langsmith-sandbox` chart's host ingress (`sandboxHost.ingress`) with TLS and set its `https://` origin as `sandboxes.external.hostIngressEndpoint`.

Copy the sandbox service-auth secret and the callback-signing JWK into the runtime chart's Secret, under the keys that chart's `serviceAuth.secretKey` (default `sandbox_service_auth_secret`) and `config.callbackSigningJwkSecretKey` name. With an external runtime the service-auth secret must be its own random value, never `api_key_salt`: that value also salts API keys and signs LangSmith's other service calls, so it must not leave this cluster, and the chart refuses to render without a dedicated secret. Generate one (for example with `openssl rand -base64 32`) and either set `sandboxes.serviceAuth.secret`, which the chart stores as `sandbox_service_auth_secret` in its managed Secret, or add it to `config.existingSecretName` under its own key and name that key in `sandboxes.serviceAuth.secretKey`. With the embedded runtime the secret still defaults to `api_key_salt`. To change the value of a running install, rotate it in three steps, and wait for both releases to finish rolling out after each one:

1. On both sides, add the new value as the previous secret: `sandboxes.serviceAuth.previousSecret` or `previousSecretKey` here, `serviceAuth.previousSecretKey` in the runtime chart.
2. On both sides, make the new value current and the old value the previous secret.
3. Remove the previous secret on both sides.

Kubernetes does not restart pods when data inside an existing Secret changes, so after editing one, restart the pods that read it (`kubectl rollout restart`). Restarting sandbox-host suspends its VMs.

Run exactly one `langsmith-sandbox` release per LangSmith install, and none while `sandboxes.external.enabled` is false. LangSmith accepts host reports from one runtime at a time and refuses any other: a refused runtime's hosts never join, and its elected `sandbox-host` pod turns not ready. To move from the embedded runtime, set `sandboxes.external.enabled=true` on this release, with its dedicated service-auth secret, first, then install `langsmith-sandbox` with the same secret. Set `sandboxes.clusterName` only at install time: renaming it hides existing sandboxes, private registries and hosts.

## Engine

LangSmith Engine is off by default. `engine.enabled=true` requires all of the following, and the chart refuses to render if any is missing:

| Setting | Why |
|---|---|
| `sandboxes.enabled=true` | Every Engine run executes in a sandbox. |
| `images.engineInsightsAgentImage.repository` = `docker.io/langchain/langsmith-insights-engine` | Engine needs the combined image serving both the `insights` and `engine` graphs. |
| `engine.encryptionKey` | Decrypts the payloads smith-go passes to the Engine. Must match smith-go's `ISSUES_AGENT_ENCRYPTION_KEY`. |
| `engine.usageSigningSecret` | Signs Engine usage reports sent to smith-go. Use at least 32 random characters. |
| `config.hostname` | Sandboxes run the `langsmith` CLI against your install from outside the cluster, so this must be externally reachable — not a loopback or in-cluster address. |

Things worth planning for before you enable it:

**LangSmith Intelligence.** `engine.intelligenceBaseUrl` defaults to `https://beacon.langchain.com/intelligence`, where Engine reports usage on your own model providers, authenticated with your license key. To also let Engine run on LangSmith Intelligence's models, set it to `https://beacon.aws.langchain.com/intelligence` and select LangSmith Intelligence under **Settings > Engine > Model providers**. Air-gapped installs with an offline license set it to `""`; Engine then runs only on your own model providers, and its usage goes out through **Settings > Usage export**.

**Sandbox nodes.** Sandboxes are Firecracker microVMs, so `sandboxes.sandboxHost.deployment.nodeSelector` must place host pods on KVM-capable nodes — bare-metal instances, or instance types with nested virtualization explicitly enabled. Sandbox images are published for `linux/amd64` only. A dedicated, tainted node pool is the usual arrangement, since rolling a sandbox-host pod suspends every microVM on it.

**Which workspace owns the sandboxes.** By default smith-go resolves the install's workspace, which works when there is exactly one non-personal organization. With more than one it declines rather than guess, and you must set `engine.sandboxTenantId` explicitly. Prefer a workspace reserved for the Engine: its sandboxes are visible to anyone with access to it.

## Observability dashboards

For tabbed Datadog and Grafana dashboards and component collection examples, see the [LangSmith observability bundle](examples/langsmith-observability/README.md). Import these definitions into your own monitoring instance. The standalone Classic-format SmithDB Grafana dashboard is available in the same bundle. All dashboard downloads now live in this bundle.

## Sandbox quotas

When `sandboxes.enabled=true`, configure limits under `sandboxes.quotas`: `maxSandboxes` (default 1000), `maxCpuCores` (200), and `maxMemoryGb` (400 GiB). These limits do not provision cluster capacity.

**Compatibility:** Configured sandbox quotas now apply to authenticated requests. Review existing Helm and organization-level quota overrides before upgrading.

## SmithDB resource tiers

`smithdb.resourceTier` sets per-replica CPU, memory, and cache size for SmithDB components. The default is `small`. Query, ingestion, and compaction worker mount a volume named `cache` at `/data` as a per-pod [generic ephemeral volume](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/ephemeral-volumes/#generic-ephemeral-volumes) on `smithdb.cache.storageClassName`, or the cluster default StorageClass when empty. Provision that StorageClass with at least 7000 IOPS and 1000 MiB/s.

Explicit component `resources` or `volumes` replace the tier values. Cache overrides must be named `cache` and use an `emptyDir` or inline ephemeral PVC; existing PVCs are unsupported. To cache on local SSD, set matching `emptyDir.sizeLimit` and `ephemeral-storage` requests and limits.

The query disk cache limit is set automatically from the PVC storage request or, for `emptyDir`, the container's `ephemeral-storage` limit. Enabled `mutations`, `runRules`, and `statsQuery` use the query tier. Replica counts and autoscaling are configured per component.

| Component | Small | Medium | Large |
|---|---|---|---|
| Query | 4 CPU, 8Gi memory, 200Gi cache | 28 CPU, 48Gi memory, 200Gi cache | 28 CPU, 50Gi memory, 1000Gi cache |
| Ingestion | 4 CPU, 8Gi memory, 100Gi cache | 16 CPU, 32Gi memory, 100Gi cache | 56 CPU, 150Gi memory, 1000Gi cache |
| Compaction | 2 CPU, 4Gi memory | 4 CPU, 8Gi memory | 8 CPU, 16Gi memory |
| Compaction worker | 8 CPU, 16Gi memory, 100Gi cache | 16 CPU, 32Gi memory, 100Gi cache | 28 CPU, 50Gi memory, 300Gi cache |
| Cluster manager | 250m CPU, 256Mi memory | 250m CPU, 256Mi memory | 2 CPU, 2Gi memory |

### Unified topology

`smithdb.topology: unified` replaces the components above with one `smithdb all` pod: no cluster manager, no compaction workers, and one cache volume. The process splits the volume between the query cache and ingestion staging, and mounts it at `/tmp` for compaction scratch. Memory and disk shares come from the pod's limits. `mutations`, `runRules`, and `statsQuery` are not supported in this topology. The pod runs one replica; use `cluster` when SmithDB needs to scale out.

| Tier | Small | Medium | Large |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unified | 4 CPU, 16Gi memory, 100Gi cache | 16 CPU, 64Gi memory, 300Gi cache | 32 CPU, 128Gi memory, 1000Gi cache |

**0.17 upgrade:** default caches switch from `emptyDir` to per-pod PVCs. Configure local SSD overrides before upgrading and rename custom volume and mount references from `local-ssd-storage` to `cache`.


## Trajectory backend

Online evaluations on trajectories use an internal gRPC listener in `platformBackend` on port `8086`, enabled when both `smithdb.enabled` and `smithdb.langsmith.query.enabled` are true. Otherwise, no trajectory listener, Service, or endpoint is configured. It shares the HTTP backend's SmithDB routing, resources, and scaling; readiness uses `/ready` while active.

The trajectory Service is cluster-internal; no public route is added. Trajectory processing can be memory intensive, consider setting [GOMEMLIMIT](https://pkg.go.dev/runtime#hdr-Environment_Variables) relative to your memory limit to mitigate OOMKilled errors for this service. If setting `GOMEMLIMIT` through `platformBackend.deployment.extraEnv`, account for the combined HTTP and trajectory workload and leave headroom below the container memory limit.

## Extra manifests

`extraManifests.enabled` defaults to `false`. Set it to `true` to let standalone self-hosted deployments declare extra ServiceAccounts and SecretProviderClasses. It needs `config.deployment.enabled=true` and the operator. The chart passes `LGP_EXTRA_MANIFESTS_ENABLED` to the operator and, through the shared ConfigMap, to the host backend, listener, and platform backend. Deployments on a remote listener are unsupported. Do not override the flag through environment values.

**Coordinated upgrade required:** enable it only with operator, host backend, listener, and platform backend images that support the feature, together with this chart's LGP CRD. This chart does not change image tags.

Rules enforced by the API and the operator:

- Only `v1/ServiceAccount` and `secrets-store.csi.x-k8s.io/v1/SecretProviderClass`, up to 16 objects.
- Every `metadata.name` must start with the deployment's resource name followed by `-`. The UI shows the exact prefix.
- SecretProviderClasses must use pod workload identity: `spec` may contain only `provider` (`aws`, `azure`, or `gcp`) and `parameters`. `secretObjects` is rejected. Azure requires `clientID` and rejects VM or pod-managed identity. GCP `auth` must be `pod-adc` when set.

Enabling the feature grants the operator SecretProviderClass `get`, `create`, `patch`, and `delete`. ServiceAccount permissions already exist. It adds no Secret or RBAC permissions. With `operator.rbac.create=false`, provision these permissions yourself.

Scope each workload identity's federation to the namespace and the full prefixed ServiceAccount name. For Azure Key Vault, install the Secrets Store CSI driver and Azure provider, and configure Azure Workload Identity and the federated identity credential. This chart does not install those prerequisites.

The operator deletes removed objects only after the deployment's rollout completes. It reports progress in the LGP `ExtraManifestsReady` condition. Turning the flag off freezes the objects and the stored configuration. Clear resources while the flag is on. Deleting a deployment garbage-collects its objects through owner references.

